<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S0.T7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Extended Data Table 7</span>: </span><span id="S0.T7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluation of synthetic data for training data augmentation in abnormality localization.<span id="S0.T7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Detection models were trained with real data alone and with synthetic data augmentation (1<math id="S0.T7.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> and 2<math id="S0.T7.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>) on the ChesX-Det dataset for thoracic pathology detection and Object-CXR dataset for foreign object detection. Performance is measured using Mean Average Precision (mAP50) and mean Average Recall (mAR50) at an IoU threshold of 0.5. All results are reported as mean <math id="S0.T7.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation over 5 independent runs, with the best performance in each row highlighted in bold. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
